{"type":"process","design":"S_001","title":"Vertex AI model approach and lifecycle","lead":"A structured machine learning pipeline turns store signals into daily replenishment forecasts.","items":[{"title":"Experimentation and feature design","text":"Data scientists explore historical point-of-sale velocity, markdown elasticity, and shelf-life decay curves inside Vertex AI Workbench to isolate the strongest predictive demand signals for each perishable department.","icon":"science-atom"},{"title":"Distributed model training","text":"Vertex AI Pipelines orchestrate automated training runs across store-item hierarchies, combining deep learning time-series algorithms with gradient boosted trees to capture seasonal and promotional patterns.","icon":"brain-circuit"},{"title":"Validation and backtesting","text":"Candidate models are evaluated against holdout store cohorts in Vertex AI Model Registry, measuring forecast error and simulated spoilage before any algorithm is approved for production.","icon":"check-circle"},{"title":"Deployment and drift monitoring","text":"Approved models deploy to Vertex AI Endpoints for batch and near-real-time inference, while Vertex AI Model Monitoring tracks feature skew and triggers automated retraining workflows.","icon":"rocket"}],"notes":"Our modeling methodology uses Vertex AI to manage the entire machine learning lifecycle from initial feature experimentation to production inference. By standardizing on Vertex AI Pipelines and Model Registry, Kroger can train localized demand models across thousands of perishable SKUs while maintaining strict governance and automated drift detection.

