{"type":"comparison","design":"N_005","title":"Static buffer thresholds versus dynamic safety stock","lead":"Replacing fixed min-max rules with dynamic buffers prevents spoilage while protecting shelf availability.","left":{"label":"Legacy approach","title":"Static min-max buffer rules","points":["Fixed presentation minimums ignore weekday versus weekend traffic swings","Manual safety stock buffers inflate backroom holding levels for slow movers","Uniform shelf-life assumptions treat hardy produce and delicate berries identically","Delayed reaction to local weather causes post-weekend perishables markdowns","High manual override volume from department managers distrusting static par levels"]},"right":{"label":"Vertex AI and Gemini","title":"Dynamic algorithmic safety stock","points":["Buffers flex daily with predicted store traffic and promotional velocity","Remaining code life and shrinkage risk are priced directly into order quantities","Gemini adjusts safety stock ahead of community events and weather shifts","Exception-based alerts let associates focus on high-value fresh merchandising"]},"notes":"Static min-max replenishment rules force stores to hold excess safety stock just to avoid stockouts, which directly drives perishable expiration. By transitioning to dynamic algorithmic buffers on Vertex AI, retail supply chains can reduce safety stock buffer requirements by 20% to 30% without depressing on-shelf product availability.

