{"type":"cards","title":"Canary rollouts and automated rollback gates","items":[{"title":"Canary traffic split","text":"Cloud Deploy shifts a small canary traffic percentage to the new revision and validates error rates over a 15-minute bake window before full promotion. This staged release pattern catches hidden regressions early and prevents service-wide degradation before any broader rollout.","icon":"workflow-path"},{"title":"Automated latency rollback","text":"Managed Service for Prometheus queries pod latency continuously and triggers an immediate automated rollback if p95 latency exceeds 180 ms during baking. Rapid automated reversals eliminate manual incident reaction time and protect end users from degraded request processing.","icon":"code-refresh"},{"title":"Staggered regional rollout","text":"Pipelines deploy to us-central1 first and enforce a mandatory soak interval before promoting manifests to us-east1, preventing simultaneous global bad deploys. This geographic separation isolates regional cluster failures and ensures multi-region service resilience.","icon":"globe-circle"}],"notes":"Canary rollouts via Cloud Deploy shift traffic progressively over a 15-minute bake window to validate new container revisions. If p95 tracking API latency exceeds 180 ms, the pipeline triggers an automated rollback, while staggered dual-region rollouts prevent simultaneous global regressions.

